= Information theory =

== Overview ==
'''Information theory''' is the mathematical study of quantifying, coding, transmitting, and processing information under uncertainty and noise.

It was formalized by Claude Shannon in the context of communication systems.

== Core quantities ==
Major concepts include:

* [[Entropy]];
* conditional entropy;
* mutual information;
* relative entropy;
* source coding;
* channel capacity;
* rate–distortion tradeoffs.

== Communication model ==
A communication system can be idealized as:

<pre>
source → encoder → channel → decoder → destination
</pre>

Noise in the channel can corrupt transmitted symbols.

Information theory studies the limits of reliable representation and communication.

== Semantics ==
Shannon's formalism intentionally abstracts away from message meaning.

Two messages with different semantic importance can have the same Shannon information statistics.

Thus:

<pre>
information theory ≠ theory of semantics
</pre>

== Coding theorems ==
Shannon's source-coding theorem relates entropy to the minimum average lossless description length.

The channel-coding theorem identifies communication rates below channel capacity for which arbitrarily low error can be approached under suitable coding assumptions.

== Related concepts ==
[[Information]], [[Entropy]], [[Probability]], [[Probability distribution]], [[Conditional probability]], [[Correlation]], and [[Uncertainty]] provide the mathematical context.

== Shannon entropy and units ==
For a discrete random variable X with possible values x and associated probabilities p(x), Shannon entropy is commonly written:

<pre>
H(X) = - sum_x p(x) log_2 p(x)
</pre>

The standard convention assigns a zero contribution to outcomes of probability zero. With a base-2 logarithm, the result is measured in bits. A fair binary source has entropy of one bit per draw; a deterministic source has entropy zero. This formal notion of uncertainty is not a statement about whether a message is accurate, meaningful, or valuable to a reader.

== Mutual information and conditions ==
Mutual information I(X;Y) measures the statistical dependence between two random variables and can be expressed as H(X) - H(X|Y) when the relevant quantities are well-defined. It is zero for statistically independent variables, but does not by itself establish a causal influence or explain the mechanism underlying a correlation.

== Channel and coding assumptions ==
Shannon's channel-capacity results concern the limiting rates of communication with suitably designed codes under specified channel models and error criteria. They do not promise reliable communication at every finite block length, latency or implementation cost. Source coding also depends on what is assumed about the source probability model and what kinds of distortion or loss are allowed.

== Scope of the mathematics ==
[[Related to::Entropy|Entropy]] appears in both information theory and statistical physics, but a Shannon entropy calculated from message probabilities is not automatically a physical thermodynamic entropy. The mathematical structure can be closely related while the modeled objects, units and physical meaning differ.

